    "captured": "2026-08-05",
    "why": "Manifold's search was pinned to contractType=BINARY, which made 30% of its labeled-wanted universe (MULTIPLE_CHOICE / MULTI_NUMERIC / NUMBER / DATE) structurally unreachable. This file pins the two facts that flip together: a contractType=ALL search returns multi-outcome rows with probability NULL and no per-answer data at all, and the per-candidate detail GET the enrichment hook already fires is where the `answers` array lives. Captured live rather than hand-built because the whole point is that the search response and the detail response disagree about what a multi-outcome market publishes.",
    "endpoints": {
      "manifold": "https://api.manifold.markets/v0/search-markets?term=US+national+average+gas+price&contractType=ALL&limit=10 and https://api.manifold.markets/v0/market/{id}"
    },
    "edits": "Search rows cut to 4 of 8 (the first two BINARY and both MULTIPLE_CHOICE, in the venue's own rank order, which parse_manifold_matches scores on); string values over 300 chars truncated, which includes each detail's textDescription. No key was renamed, added or removed and no probability was touched - key PRESENCE is what the search assertions read, and the expected top-answer tuples are hand-copied FROM these figures.",
    "payloads": {
      "search_all": "The same live term the ENRICHMENT_SPEC measured: 6 BINARY + 2 MULTIPLE_CHOICE under contractType=ALL, 6 BINARY under BINARY. Every row carries outcomeType, closeTime, totalLiquidity, volume and uniqueBettorCount; the two MULTIPLE_CHOICE rows carry probability=null and NO `answers` key, so a multi-outcome row reaching the pool off the search alone has no price to render.",
      "detail_multiple_choice": "E0ONq9czPd, 'How high will the US national average gas price get in 2026?' - one of the labeled-wanted rows the BINARY pin excluded. 17 answers, shouldAnswersSumToOne=false, and 10 already resolved YES at probability exactly 1.0, so the top three are a TIE: the fixture only pins a deterministic top-3 because the sort is stable and keeps the array's own order among equals. Market-level probability is null while isResolved is false.",
      "detail_multi_numeric": "85NS9Ut65L, a bucketed MULTI_NUMERIC market on the same quantity: 7 answers, shouldAnswersSumToOne=true, answers carrying an extra `midpoint` key, top three at 0.508 / 0.247 / 0.160. This is the receipt that ONE code path covers all four non-BINARY types - the array shape is the same as MULTIPLE_CHOICE's, and NUMBER ('9000-10000' buckets) and DATE ('Nov-Dec 2026' buckets) were spot-checked live to match.",
      "detail_binary": "sZzIqycRcS, a BINARY market's detail, present to prove the discriminator is real rather than cosmetic: a BINARY detail carries NO `answers` key at all and a populated market-level probability, so reading the array is enough to tell the two shapes apart without a second outcomeType read."
    }
